    "is_active": false,
    "site_description": "Ram Bagh, originally known as Bagh-i Nur Afshan (Light-Scattering Garden), is the oldest surviving Mughal garden in Agra. Created under the patronage of Empress Nur Jahan in the reign of Jahangir, it exemplifies early Mughal garden design focused on water symmetry and riverfront terrace planning. The garden includes terraced walkways, sunken water channels, tahkhanas (cool underground chambers), pavilions with intricate paintings, and symbolic elements like muqarnas and fish-scale patterned chutes. Though altered during British rule, many features have been restored by the Archaeological Survey of India. Its rich iconography and connection to Jahangir and Nur Jahan reflect both power and paradise motifs.",
    "docId": "ram-bagh-003"
  },
